From c2bf05fbb134ee977ea649dec67e308fcda0b20a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:45:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kk: share compiled pipeline states between equal pipelines

The compiled MSL library is shared per source (a previous patch), but the Metal
pipeline state built on top of it - the part Metal spends its time on - was
created once per Vulkan pipeline, and a title rebuilds the same pipelines over
and over. In one Dragon's Dogma 2 first run the compile log
(MESA_KK_DEBUG=compiles) has 26,452 program compiles for 4,594 distinct
programs; 21,178 of them are render pipelines that repeat the same shaders, and
they cost 529 s of the run's ~1,930 s of Metal time (they are not Metal cache
hits: each repeat lasts ~25 ms). The host benchmark shows the same shape in
isolation, one program compiled 197 times in one process.

Build a key out of what the Metal pipeline is built from and use one state for
one key:

  - kk_metal_render_key: the two libraries (kk_get_msl_library hands out one
    canonical instance per source, so equal programs get equal pointers), their
    entry points, the primitive topology class, the color attachment formats,
    the raster sample count and the maximum vertex amplification - exactly what
    kk_compile_graphics_pipeline puts on the MTL4RenderPipelineDescriptor.
  - kk_metal_compute_key: the library, its entry point and the threadgroup size,
    exactly what mtl_new_compute_pipeline_state takes.

The two tables are per device, hold their keys (and the states) for its
lifetime, and are shared by the compile pool. They are single-flight: a worker
that finds a key already being compiled waits for it (cnd_wait) instead of
compiling the same program a second time, which is worth doing because a title
starts whole bursts of pipelines with the same shaders at once - without it the
table stops duplicate work only after the fact. Measured on the benchmark
(MODULES=200 REPEAT=24 THREADS=8): 664 program compiles before, 453 after, with
453 distinct - the duplicates are gone rather than merely cheap.

